<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">cargo</ansi>

Your company shares one pool of carrying capacity. Everything counts
toward it by its actual weight in grams: what you personally carry and
wear, what every living companion with you carries and wears, and
whatever sits in the company's shared cargo. A companion who isn't
currently with you doesn't count.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Moving things into cargo</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cargo put [item]</ansi>
  Moves an item from your own pack into the shared cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">cargo take [item]</ansi>
  Moves an item from the shared cargo back into your pack.

Moving something between your pack and cargo doesn't change your total
load, so it's never blocked. Cargo is just where you keep things you
don't need on hand.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Capacity and being overloaded</ansi>

Your capacity is a base amount, plus a bonus if you have a mount
stabled (<ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>). Once your total load passes about three
quarters of that capacity, you get worse at everything that depends on
moving:

  - Journeys along a route take longer.
  - Walking room to room strains you and your company more
    (<ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>).

It gets worse in stages the heavier you load up: past three quarters
of capacity, routes and strain both cost a little more; past nine
tenths, noticeably more; and at or over full capacity, a lot more.
None of this ever speeds you up — an underloaded company travels at its
ordinary pace, not faster.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cargo</ansi>
  Shows your total load against capacity, the split between your own
  gear, your companions' gear, and cargo, any travel or strain
  modifier that's currently in effect, and what's in the cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">cargo put [item]</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">cargo take [item]</ansi>

See also: <ansi fg="command">help encumbrance</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help inventory</ansi>.
